{"name":"Cricket Green","hex":"#6a7b6b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cricket","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An insect in the order Orthoptera, especially family Gryllidae, that makes a chirping sound by rubbing its wing casings against combs on its hind legs.\n In the form crickets: absolute silence; no communication.","In the form crickets: absolute silence; no communication."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A game played outdoors with bats and a ball between two teams of eleven, popular in England and many Commonwealth countries.","An act that is fair and sportsmanlike."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cricket"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Cricket Green","blocks":[{"text":"Cricket Ernest Green is a fictional character and the central protagonist of the American animated television series Big City Greens, created by the Houghton Brothers. He is a 10-year-old country boy and the only son of Bill and Nancy Green, the younger brother of Tilly Green, and grandson of Alice Green, the deceased Ernest Green, and Nick Mulligan. He is voiced by series co-creator Chris Houghton and first appeared in the series premiere \"Space Chicken\", on June 18, 2018."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/53/Cricket_Green.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket_Green"},{"title":"Cricket Green (ward)","blocks":[{"text":"Cricket Green is an electoral ward in the London Borough of Merton. The ward was first used in the 2002 elections and elects three councillors to Merton London Borough Council."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket_Green_(ward)"},{"title":"Cricket","blocks":[{"text":"Cricket is a bat-and-ball game that is played between two teams of eleven players on a field, at the centre of which is a 22-yard pitch with a wicket at each end, each comprising two bails balanced on three stumps. Two players from the batting team, the striker and nonstriker, stand in front of either wicket holding bats, while one player from the fielding team, the bowler, bowls the ball toward the striker's wicket from the opposite end of the pitch. The striker's goal is to hit the bowled ball with the bat and then switch places with the nonstriker, with the batting team scoring one run for each of these swaps. Runs are also scored when the ball reaches the boundary of the field or when the ball is bowled illegally."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7a/Pollock_to_Hussey.jpg/330px-Pollock_to_Hussey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cricket"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Comikaze_Expo_2011_-_Chris_and_Shane_Houghton%2C_creators_of_Reed_Gunther_%286325367668%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Comikaze_Expo_2011_-_Chris_and_Shane_Houghton%2C_creators_of_Reed_Gunther_%286325367668%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Comikaze_Expo_2011_-_Chris_and_Shane_Houghton%2C_creators_of_Reed_Gunther_%286325367668%29_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Comikaze_Expo_2011_-_Chris_and_Shane_Houghton%2C_creators_of_Reed_Gunther_%286325367668%29_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chris Houghton voices Cricket Green.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComikaze_Expo_2011_-_Chris_and_Shane_Houghton%2C_creators_of_Reed_Gunther_(6325367668)_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg/250px-Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg/250px-Cantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A medieval \"club ball\" game involving an underarm bowl towards a batter. Ball catchers are shown positioning themselves to catch a ball. Detail from the Canticles of Holy Mary, 13th century.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACantigas_de_Santa_Mar%C3%ADa_-_Xogo_da_pelota.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg/500px-Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg/500px-Historical_cricket_bat_art.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Evolution of the cricket bat. The original \"hockey stick\" (left) evolved into the straight bat from c. 1760, when pitched delivery bowling began.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHistorical_cricket_bat_art.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg/500px-Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg/1280px-Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a9/Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg/500px-Francis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_%281768%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Young Cricketer (Francis Cotes, 1768)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFrancis_Cotes_-_The_young_cricketer_(1768).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/First_photo_of_a_cricket_match_by_Roger_Fenton.jpg/500px-First_photo_of_a_cricket_match_by_Roger_Fenton.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https: